{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"agent_message","text":"The G_tiling approach change gives negligible difference. Let me try a more meaningful optimization: computing the v-projection in a layout that allows direct use without permuting.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about the biggest potential wins. The projections are ~60% of runtime. We need q, k, v all projected. Unless we can reduce the projection count (we can't — they all have different weights), we need to make each projection faster.\n\nOne idea: use `torch.nn.functional.linear` which might be optimized differently. Another: transpose the weights to (C, D) format first so the matmul is x @ w instead of x @ w.T.\n\nLet me also explore whether computing q and k projections differently helps. Actually, I just remembered — we only need `q @ k^T`, not q and k separately. `q = x @ wq^T`, `k = x @ wk^T`, so `logits = x @ wq^T @ wk @ x^T / sqrt(D)`. But that's the K_h formulation which is more expensive.\n\nWait, what if we do `qk_weight = wq.reshape(HD, C) @ wk.reshape(HD, C).T / sqrt(D)`? That's (HD, HD) size = (1280, 1280) for case 3. Then `logits = x @ qk_weight @ x^T`. No, that doesn't work per-head.\n\nLet me try one more creative optimization: can we compute p0 and p1 softmax more efficiently by reusing the max and sum computations? Probably not worth the effort.\n\nLet me try a different approach — use `torch.scaled_dot_product_attention` for the p0 computation, which might be highly optimized on CPU.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cd /workspace/solution && python3 -c \"\nimport torch\nimport time\nimport math\nimport statistics\n\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\nB, L, C, H, D = 1, 256, 1280, 20, 64\nBH = B * H\nHD = H * D\n\ntorch.manual_seed(42)\nx = torch.randn(B, L, C)\nwq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\nwk = torch.randn(H, D, C)\nxr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\ninv_sqrt_d = 1.0 / math.sqrt(D)\n\nq = (xr @ wq.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\nk = (xr @ wk.reshape(HD, C).T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n\ndef time_fn(fn, n=30):\n    for _ in range(5): fn()\n    times = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        fn()\n        times.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n    return statistics.median(times)\n\n# Manual p0 = softmax(q @ k''^T / sqrt(D))\ndef manual_p0():\n    logits = q @ k.transpose(-1, -2) * inv_sqrt_d\n    return logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n\n# SDPA\ndef sdpa_p0():\n    # SDPA expects (B, H, L, D)\n    return torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, torch.ones(B, H, L, D))\n    # No, SDPA computes softmax(qk''^T/sqrt(d)) @ v. We just want the attention weights.\n\n# Actually we need the softmax probabilities, not the output. \n# SDPA returns output, not weights. So not directly useful.\n\n# But wait - do we really need p0 explicitly? \n# p_mix = p0 + beta*(p1 - p0) = (1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1\n# We use p_mix for: p_mix @ v, p_mix @ G (for norm)\n# We use p1 for: p1 @ G (for norm)\n# We never use p0 alone except to compute p_mix.\n# \n# So we compute: p0, p1 explicitly, then p_mix\n# Total softmaxes: 2\n# \n# If we could compute p_mix @ v directly as (1-beta)*p0@v + beta*p1@v, \n# and p_mix @ G directly as (1-beta)*p0@G + beta*p1@G,\n# we'\"'d save the p_mix allocation but add more bmm ops.\n# p_mix allocation: BH*L*L = 20*256*256*4 = 5.2 MB - negligible.\n\n# Let me think about what's actually expensive.\n# Two softmaxes at ~6ms each for case 3 = 12ms\n# Two norm bmms at ~6ms each = 12ms  \n# Three projections at ~7ms each = 21ms\n# logits bmm + p_mix@v bmm = ~3ms\n# Other = ~3ms\n# Total = ~51ms\n\n# Can we reduce to 1 softmax? Only if alpha=1.0 (case 6).\n# For general case, we need both p0 and p1.\n\n# What about computing p1 more efficiently?\n# p1 = softmax(a_p * A + a_m * A\"'^T)\n# We already have A. Computing A''^T is just a view (transpose).\n# The p1_logits = a_p * A + a_m * A''^T is one mul + one add = ~1ms, negligible.\n\n# OK here'\"'s an idea: can we use the ONEDNN fast path?\n# If we use 4D tensors with certain layouts, MKLDNN might be faster.\n# Let me check if making q and k channels-last helps.\n\ndef test_cl():\n    qc = q.to(memory_format=torch.channels_last)\n    kc = k.to(memory_format=torch.channels_last)\n    logits = qc @ kc.transpose(-1, -2)\n    return logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n\n# channels_last might not apply to 4D attention\nprint(f'manual p0: {time_fn(manual_p0):.3f} ms')\n\n# Let me try something more impactful: \n# What if we compute logits and p0 in-place to save memory allocations?\n# We need: logits -> compute p0 -> compute p1_logits (needs logits) -> compute p1\n# So we can't overwrite logits until after p1_logits is computed.\n# But we can:\n# 1. Compute logits\n# 2. Compute p0 (needs logits) \n# 3. Reuse logits memory for p1_logits\n# 4. Compute p1 from p1_logits\n# Wait, p1_logits = a_p * logits + a_m * logits\"'^T\n# We can'\"'t do this in-place on logits because of the transpose overlap.\n\n# Actually, we CAN do: compute p0 first, then overwrite logits with p1_logits.\n# But p1_logits = a_p * original_logits + a_m * original_logits\"'^T\n# The problem: if we multiply in-place first, then we need original for the transpose part.\n# Solution: save original logits''^T? No, same memory.\n# \n# Alternative: p1_logits = 0.5 * (logits + logits''^T) + 0.5 * alpha * (logits - logits''^T)\n# = symmetrize + alpha * anti-symmetrize\n# Still needs both logits and logits''^T.\n# \n# Can'\"'t avoid the allocation for p1_logits. It's BH*L*L = small anyway.\n\nprint('Looking for the next big optimization...')\n\n# KEY INSIGHT: We compute norm via p @ G @ p\"'^T (diagonal), \n# where G = x @ x''^T. \n# But we'\"'re computing full p@G (BH*L*L) just to get the diagonal (BH*L).\n# That's BH*L*L computation for BH*L output - very wasteful\"'!\n# \n# Can we compute just the diagonal?\n# norm[i] = sum_jk p[i,j] * G[j,k] * p[i,k]\n# = sum_j p[i,j] * (G @ p[i,:]''^T)[j]\n# = row-wise dot product of p and p@G\n# \n# To compute this efficiently, we need p@G which is what we'\"'re computing.\n# The bmm computes all of p@G, then we reduce.\n# \n# Alternative: G is symmetric. norm[i] = sum_j sum_k p_ij * G_jk * p_ik\n# = vec(p_i)\"'^T @ G @ vec(p_i)  -- quadratic form\n# \n# For each head, this is L dot products of size L (with G in between).\n# Total: BH * L''^2 = same as bmm. So no savings.\n# \n# The bmm approach is already optimal for this computation.\n\" 2>&1\n'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
