{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_61","type":"agent_message","text":"Negligible difference. Let me think more deeply about where a real optimization could come from.\n\nThe three projections dominate. What if we compute q and k together differently?\n\nActually, let me re-examine the math. We need:\n- logits = q @ k^T / sqrt(D) where q = x @ wq^T, k = x @ wk^T\n- p_mix @ v where v = x @ wv^T\n\nWhat if we compute `wq @ wk^T` first (per head), which is (D, D)? Then `logits = x @ (wq^T @ wk) @ x^T / sqrt(D)`. No, that's per-sample... wait no, per head.\n\nActually: `q[b,h] = x[b] @ wq[h]^T` (L x D), `k[b,h] = x[b] @ wk[h]^T` (L x D)\n`logits[b,h] = q[b,h] @ k[b,h]^T / sqrt(D)` (L x L)\n\n= `x[b] @ wq[h]^T @ wk[h] @ x[b]^T / sqrt(D)` (L x L)\n\nIf we precompute `K_h = wq[h]^T @ wk[h]` (C x C) per head:\n- Cost per head: C*D*C = C²*D = 1280²*64 = 105M, times H=20 = 2.1B FLOPs\n- Way more than current q-proj + k-proj: 2*BL*C*D = 2*256*1280*64*20 = 839M\n\nSo worse. What about v-projection? Same issue.\n\nThe projections are fundamentally BL*C*HD each. We can't beat the FLOP count.\n\nLet me try one more idea: use MKL's batched gemm via bmm for per-head projections instead of one big matmul. For per-head, each is (L, C) @ (C, D) = L*C*D FLOPS, H heads.\n</think>\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cd /workspace/solution && python3 -c \"\nimport torch\nimport time\nimport math\nimport statistics\n\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\ncases = [\n    (1, 64, 512, 8, 64, 1.05, 5.0),\n    (1, 128, 640, 10, 64, 1.1, 4.0),\n    (1, 128, 1280, 20, 64, 1.15, 3.0),\n    (1, 256, 1280, 20, 64, 1.05, 7.5),\n    (1, 256, 640, 10, 64, 1.2, 5.0),\n    (2, 128, 512, 8, 64, 1.0, 5.0),\n]\n\nfrom method import attention as current_attention\n\n# New approach: For the v projection + p_mix@v, can we reorder as (p_mix @ x) @ wv''^T\n# and fuse with norm computation?\n# \n# Actually, let me try a completely different approach for norms:\n# Instead of Gram, compute norms of p_mix @ v directly? No, we need norm of u1 = p1 @ x, not p1 @ v.\n# \n# WAIT. The problem says:\n# u0 = P0 @ x (in x-space, shape B*H*L*C)\n# u1 = P1 @ x\n# m = u0 + beta*(u1-u0) = p_mix @ x\n# ratio = clamp(||u1|| / ||m||, 0.25, 4.0)\n# output = (r * m) @ wv''^T\n# \n# We currently compute norms via Gram to avoid p@x (expensive when C>L).\n# But what about computing output as p_mix @ v directly (cheap when D<<C)?\n# AND computing norms via Gram (also cheap)?\n# \n# Current = Gram for norms + p_mix @ v for output\n# This is already optimal.\n#\n# Let me try: can we compute p1 + p_mix + ratio more efficiently?\n# \n# What about:\n# p_mix = p0 + beta*(p1-p0)\n# norm_m''^2 = ||p_mix @ x||''^2 = p_mix @ G @ p_mix''^T (diag)\n# \n# Let me try a version where we compute both norm bmms together:\n# by stacking p1 and p_mix as (2*BH, L, L) and doing one bmm with (2*BH, L, L) G.\n# \n# Actually let me try something completely different.\n# What if we use einsum optimized paths?\n# \n# No, we already showed einsum is slower.\n# \n# Let me focus on Python-level micro-optimizations that add up:\n# 1. Precompute all shape constants\n# 2. Minimize tensor operations / allocations\n# 3. Use in-place ops aggressively\n# 4. Avoid unnecessary .contiguous() calls\n\ndef opt_attention(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta):\n    B, L, C = x.shape\n    H = wq.shape[0]\n    D = wq.shape[1]\n    inv_sqrt_d = math.rsqrt(D)\n    HD = H * D\n    BH = B * H\n    xr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\n    \n    wqr = wq.reshape(HD, C)\n    wkr = wk.reshape(HD, C)\n    wvr = wv.reshape(HD, C)\n    \n    q = (xr @ wqr.T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n    k = (xr @ wkr.T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n    q.mul_(inv_sqrt_d)\n    \n    logits = torch.bmm(q, k.transpose(1, 2))\n    p0 = logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n    \n    if alpha == 1.0:\n        v = (xr @ wvr.T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n        y = torch.bmm(p0, v)\n    else:\n        half = 0.5\n        a_p = half + half * alpha\n        a_m = half - half * alpha\n        p1_logits = logits.mul(a_p).add_(logits.transpose(1, 2), alpha=a_m)\n        p1 = p1_logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n        p_mix = torch.lerp(p0, p1, beta)\n        \n        G = x @ x.transpose(-1, -2)\n        G_tiled = G.unsqueeze(1).expand(B, H, L, L).reshape(BH, L, L)\n        \n        p1G = torch.bmm(p1, G_tiled)\n        norm1 = p1G.mul_(p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        pmG = torch.bmm(p_mix, G_tiled)\n        normm = pmG.mul_(p_mix).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        ratio = norm1.div_(normm).clamp_(0.25, 4.0)\n        \n        v = (xr @ wvr.T).view(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().view(BH, L, D)\n        y = torch.bmm(p_mix, v).mul_(ratio)\n    \n    return y.view(B, H, L, D).transpose(1, 2).flatten(2).contiguous()\n\n# Verify\nall_ok = True\nfor i, (B, L, C, H, D, alpha, beta) in enumerate(cases):\n    torch.manual_seed(i*100)\n    x = torch.randn(B, L, C)\n    wq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wk = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wv = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    y1 = current_attention(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n    y2 = opt_attention(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n    ok = torch.allclose(y1, y2, atol=3e-5, rtol=3e-4)\n    if not ok:\n        print(f'\"'Case {i}: FAIL, diff={(y1-y2).abs().max():.2e}')\n        all_ok = False\nprint(f'All correct: {all_ok}')\n\n# Time\ndef time_fn(fn, n=11):\n    for _ in range(3): fn()\n    times = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        fn()\n        times.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n    return statistics.median(times)\n\nfor i, (B, L, C, H, D, alpha, beta) in enumerate(cases):\n    torch.manual_seed(i*100)\n    x = torch.randn(B, L, C)\n    wq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wk = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wv = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    tc = time_fn(lambda: current_attention(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta))\n    to = time_fn(lambda: opt_attention(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta))\n    print(f'Case {i}: {tc:.3f} -> {to:.3f} ms ({(tc-to)/tc*100:+.2f}%)')\n\\\" 2>&1\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
