The three linear projections dominate (~60% of time) and are limited by BLAS efficiency. Gram-based norms are the right approach (3x fewer FLOPs than direct p@x). Softmax and elementwise ops are memory-bound.\n\nLet me try one more meaningful optimization before running the full evaluation: computing both norm bmms with a single larger stacked bmm, but done more efficiently by reusing the G_tiled allocation. I'll also clean up the code for maximum performance.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace/solution && python3 -c \\\"\nimport torch\nimport time\nimport math\nimport statistics\n\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\nB, L, C, H, D = 1, 256, 1280, 20, 64\nalpha = 1.05\nbeta = 7.5\nBH = B * H\nHD = H * D\n\ntorch.manual_seed(42)\nx = torch.randn(B, L, C)\nxr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\np1 = torch.randn(BH, L, L).softmax(dim=-1)\npm = torch.randn(BH, L, L).softmax(dim=-1)\n\nG = x @ x.transpose(-1, -2)\n\ndef time_fn(fn, n=30):\n    for _ in range(5): fn()\n    times = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        fn()\n        times.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n    return statistics.median(times)\n\n# Current: two separate bmm calls with tiled G\ndef two_bmms():\n    G_tiled = G.unsqueeze(1).expand(B, H, L, L).reshape(BH, L, L)\n    p1G = torch.bmm(p1, G_tiled)\n    n1 = p1G.mul_(p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n    pmG = torch.bmm(pm, G_tiled)\n    nm = pmG.mul_(pm).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n    return n1, nm\n\n# Try: use baddbmm style? No, that adds two bmms.\n# Try: single bmm with both p matrices stacked\ndef stacked_bmm():\n    p_stack = torch.cat([p1, pm], dim=0)  # (2*BH, L, L)\n    G_stack = G.repeat(2 * H, 1, 1)  # (2*BH, L, L) - B=1 so just repeat\n    result = torch.bmm(p_stack, G_stack)\n    n1 = result[:BH].mul_(p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n    nm = result[BH:].mul_(pm).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n    return n1, nm\n\nn1a, nma = two_bmms()\nn1b, nmb = stacked_bmm()\nprint(f'N1 match: {torch.allclose(n1a, n1b)}')\nprint(f'Nm match: {torch.allclose(nma, nmb)}')\nprint(f'Two bmms: {time_fn(two_bmms):.3f} ms')\nprint(f'Stacked bmm: {time_fn(stacked_bmm):.3f} ms')\n\n# Try: in-place approach - compute pmG in same buffer\n# Actually, let me try computing norms of p1 and p_mix in a single fused way\n# Using: n1 = sum_j (p1_j * sum_k G_jk * p1_k)\n# This is the quadratic form. The bmm approach is already optimal.\n\n# Let me try: 4D matmul with broadcasting\np1_4d = p1.view(B, H, L, L)\npm_4d = pm.view(B, H, L, L)\nG_4d = G.unsqueeze(1)  # (B, 1, L, L)\n\ndef two_4d():\n    p1G = p1_4d @ G_4d\n    n1 = p1G.mul_(p1_4d).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).view(BH, L, 1)\n    pmG = pm_4d @ G_4d\n    nm = pmG.mul_(pm_4d).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True).view(BH, L, 1)\n    return n1, nm\n\nn1c, nmc = two_4d()\nprint(f'4D match: {torch.allclose(n1a, n1c)}')\nprint(f'Two 4D: {time_fn(two_4d):.3f} ms')\n\n# The bmm approach is the fastest. Let's accept that and move on.\n# \n# Let me instead try one big idea: can we avoid the v-projection entirely\n# by using the Woodbury formula or some matrix identity?\n# \n# Output = (ratio * p_mix) @ (x @ wv\"'^T) = (ratio * p_mix @ x) @ wv''^T\n# If we compute p_mix @ x for output, we pay BH*L*L*C = 1677M \n# vs v-proj (419M) + p_mix@v (84M) = 503M.\n# 3.3x more expensive. Not worth it.\n#\n# Final thought: what if C is small and D is large? Then direct p@x might be better.\n# But D is always 64 in all cases, and C >= 512 >= L for small cases.\n# For case 0: L=64, C=512. L < C, so Gram is still better (L''^2 < L*C).\n# For all cases: L <= 256, C >= 512, so L < C always. Gram is always better.\n\nprint('\"'\\\\\\\\nGram approach confirmed optimal for all cases')\n\\\" 2>&1\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
